        {
          "type": "footer",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.945,
            0.889,
            0.966
          ],
          "content": "— Textbook of Science for Grade 8"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.256,
            0.084,
            0.47,
            0.104
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Electrostatic force"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.117,
            0.676,
            0.142
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.6: Let us experiment"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.164,
            0.35,
            0.268
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.087,
                0.281,
                0.34,
                0.317
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.8: Charged plastic scale attracting small paper pieces"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_3.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.164,
            0.884,
            0.203
          ],
          "content": "Take a plastic scale or a plastic straw, a piece of polythene, and small pieces of paper."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.206,
            0.875,
            0.227
          ],
          "content": "z Rub plastic scale/straw vigorously with polythene."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.231,
            0.882,
            0.269
          ],
          "content": "Do not touch the rubbed part with your hand or any metal object."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.361,
            0.273,
            0.883,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": "z Now, bring it close to the small pieces of paper placed on a table, taking care not to touch the paper pieces (Fig. 5.8). Do you notice something surprising?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.334,
            0.883,
            0.39
          ],
          "content": "The paper pieces get pulled towards the plastic scale/straw and stick to it when it is brought close to paper pieces. Why does this happen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.391,
            0.884,
            0.521
          ],
          "content": "When two objects of certain materials are rubbed together, electrical charges build up on their surfaces. These charges are called **static charges** as they do not move by themselves. The object that acquires static charges is said to be a **charged object**. A charged object attracts, that is, exerts a force on uncharged objects made of certain materials, such as small pieces of paper. This force comes into play even when the objects are not in contact."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.254,
            0.522,
            0.883,
            0.561
          ],
          "content": "Let us do another activity with objects made of different materials."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.572,
            0.676,
            0.596
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Activity 5.7: Let us experiment"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.061,
            0.624,
            0.336,
            0.731
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_13.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.615,
            0.882,
            0.653
          ],
          "content": "z Take two balloons, a length of thread, and a woollen cloth."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.059,
            0.736,
            0.336,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.081,
                0.844,
                0.314,
                0.893
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 5.9: (a) Two uncharged balloons; (b) Two charged balloons repelling each other"
            }
          ],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_8_image_15.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.35,
            0.658,
            0.882,
            0.697
          ],
          "content": "Inflate two balloons and hang them in such a way that they do not touch each other as shown in Fig. 5.9a."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.7,
            0.883,
            0.758
          ],
          "content": "Rub both balloons with the woollen cloth and release them. Be careful not to touch the rubbed balloons with your fingers. What do you observe?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.333,
            0.761,
            0.882,
            0.8
          ],
          "content": "We observe that the balloons move away from each other as if they are repelling each other (Fig. 5.9b)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.349,
            0.801,
            0.883,
            0.858
          ],
          "content": "z Now bring the woollen cloth used for rubbing the balloons close to one of the rubbed balloons. What happens?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.332,
            0.861,
            0.883,
            0.9
          ],
          "content": "They move towards each other as if they are attracting each other. What do we infer from these observations?"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.07,
            0.924,
            0.094,
            0.938
          ],
          "content": "70"
        },
